          "text": "Definition fields exactly: name,revision=1,params:[{name,type}],dependencies:\nsymbol->pin,steps:[{id,type,deps,node}],order:[step IDs],result:expression,result_type.\nOmit identity only on admission; use tool-returned immutable pins afterward.\nTypes Int64/Bool/Unit. Expressions {ref:name},{const:int/bool/null},{add:[e,e]},\n{le:[e,e]},{eq:[e,e]}. Nodes {op:value,expr:e}, {op:check,test:e,site:e,code:CODE},\n{op:atom,codec:uint8},{op:end},{op:compose,symbol:name,identity:pin,args:{param:ref/const}}.\ndeps exactly enumerate all referenced local IDs/parameters, including check site.\nCheck/end return Unit; atom returns Int64; value/compose types follow expressions.\nOrder is an exact dependency-respecting permutation; no shadowing or cycles.\nRoot params[] and result_type Int64; final root encoded UInt16BE. Max32 steps,\n8 definitions/closure,64 expanded nodes,depth4. Admission does static validation,\nnot functional acceptance. Retrieval provides closed definitions and dependency\nimpact. Validate/execute take admitted closed-root identity; execute also input_hex.\nUse actual MCP tools. No host JSON-action broker exists in this round.\n\nRequirement: Read x UInt8, check input end before computation. Check x<=176 (RANGE_HIGH at x) BEFORE 32<=x (RANGE_LOW at x). These bounds are exclusive relative to 31 and177: reject x=31 and x=177. Return (x+x)+14. No interval_fee relation has these semantics unchanged.\nAll additions are checked Int64. Construct Entry()->Int64 and emit UInt16BE. Use assigned library only if appropriate. Task-local definitions are permitted but never enter reusable vocabulary. Read-only assigned library index: [{\"identity\": null, \"name\": null, \"non_applicability\": \"Not applicable when the interval must constrain the fee-adjusted result instead of the original x: with x=10, floor=0, ceiling=10, fee=1, this composition returns 11, while a requirement to keep x+fee within [0,10] would reject it.\", \"params\": [{\"name\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"floor\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"ceiling\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"fee\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}], \"relation\": \"interval_fee\", \"result_type\": \"Int64\", \"semantics\": \"For Int64 parameters in the exact order x, floor, ceiling, fee: first check floor <= x, failing with RANGE_LOW at site x if false; next check x <= ceiling, failing with RANGE_HIGH at site x if false; only after both checks pass, evaluate the checked Int64 addition x + fee and return it. Both checks refer to the original x, not the fee-adjusted result. If both range conditions are false, RANGE_LOW takes precedence. Addition uses the existing add expression and its existing checked arithmetic semantics.\"}, {\"identity\": null, \"name\": null, \"non_applicability\": \"Not applicable when only the combined sum must lie between 2*floor and cap, without requiring each input to meet floor: for x=-1, y=5, floor=0, cap=10, the sum 4 meets that combined bound, but this composition fails with FIRST_LOW at site -1.\", \"params\": [{\"name\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"y\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"floor\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"cap\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}], \"relation\": \"pair_envelope\", \"result_type\": \"Int64\", \"semantics\": \"For Int64 parameters in the exact order x, y, floor, cap: first check floor <= x, failing with FIRST_LOW at site x if false; then check floor <= y, failing with SECOND_LOW at site y if false. After both checks pass, evaluate x + y using the existing checked Int64 add semantics. Check that this sum <= cap, failing with TOTAL_HIGH at site y if false. Return the sum only after that check passes. The stated check order determines failure precedence; the cap constrains the combined sum, not either input individually.\"}, {\"identity\": null, \"name\": null, \"non_applicability\": \"Not applicable when limit constrains the doubled load rather than the original y: x=3, y=4, expected=3, limit=5, bias=1 passes this composition and returns 12, whereas a requirement that y+y <= limit would reject it.\", \"params\": [{\"name\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"y\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"expected\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"limit\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}, {\"name\": \"bias\", \"type\": \"Int64\"}], \"relation\": \"echo_charge\", \"result_type\": \"Int64\", \"semantics\": \"For Int64 parameters in exact order x, y, expected, limit, bias: first check x == expected, failing with KEY_MISMATCH at site x if false; then check y <= limit, failing with LOAD_HIGH at site y if false. Only after both checks pass, compute and return ((x + y) + y) + bias, with each addition using existing checked Int64 add semantics in that exact left-associated order. KEY_MISMATCH takes precedence when both checks would fail. The limit constrains the original y, not the doubled load or final result; bias is applied only in the final addition.\"}]\nFetch full assigned definitions/templates using lykoi_library(relation). A has an empty reusable library. Expanded templates provide underlying primitive steps for inlining; they have no reusable pins. Use actual admission/validation tools; selftest only author-chosen inputs (max12). Seal first complete candidate with lykoi_submit. You may self-correct and seal up to two more submissions. No acceptance feedback will be given. Stop after final submission.",
